Send your manuscript out for someone to mark up in Word or LibreOffice, then bring their
comments and edits back into the project instead of a second, disconnected copy.

## Send a Word or LibreOffice file

Export with `Ctrl+Shift+E`, and under *Format* choose *Word* (`.docx`) or *LibreOffice*
(`.odt`). Those two carry your comments out.

## Prepare the file for its return

Each export style has a *Sending to an editor* page of its own:

- *Include comments* writes your comment threads into the file.
- *Include round-trip markers* lets Skribisto recognize your chapters when the file comes back.
  Leave this on to update the existing manuscript instead of importing another copy.

To reach them, open *Settings ▸ Compile & Export ▸ Export Formats*. A built-in style cannot be
edited, so press *Duplicate*, then *Edit* on your copy, then *Sending to an editor*.

## What your reader does

Nothing special. They comment and edit in Word or LibreOffice as usual, then send the file
back.

## Bringing the file back

*Work ▸ Import from ▸ Documents (Markdown, Word, ODT)…* opens the import wizard. See
[Import documents](:help-import-documents) for the steps before *Merge*. The round trip
happens on *Merge*.

Import one reader’s copy at a time. Importing several together adds another book instead
of combining their changes with yours.

## Reading the Merge step

Choose the destination containing the original manuscript. Skribisto matches the returning
chapters and scenes to the ones already there.

The *Status* column says what happened:

- *Same*, *They edited it*, *You edited it* or *Both edited it*, when the file carries the
  original wording to compare both sides against.
- *Same* or *Differs*, when it does not.
- *New* for an item only the file has, *Not in this file* for one only you have.
- *Moved* takes the place of those when the file puts a paired item somewhere else, so use
  *Compare* to see whether the text changed as well.

If your reader moved a chapter, move it yourself in the outline.

*What to do* offers *Comments only* (their notes, your wording), *Take this version*, *Keep
mine*, *Add as new* and *Skip*. Review the selected action for each item. Some statuses limit the choices:

- *New* offers *Add as new* or *Skip*.
- *Not in this file* offers *Keep mine* alone: a chapter missing from their copy is no reason
  to delete yours.

## Taking part of a rewrite

*Compare* shows one item's two versions as one marked-up text: struck through is what they
removed, underlined what they added. Below it sits a tick box per changed paragraph, each
saying what it does: *Take this new paragraph*, *Take this rewrite* or *Drop this paragraph*.
Tick the ones you accept, and every paragraph you leave keeps your wording.

*Comments only* also applies any paragraph changes you checked in *Compare*. Leave those
boxes unchecked to import only comments. *Take this version* also uses your paragraph choices;
choose *Keep mine* to keep your own text.

## Tracked changes

A file that comes back mid-revision arrives with its changes already accepted: insertions kept,
deletions dropped. The *Review* step reports it, naming who made the changes when the file
records it.

Accepting or rejecting them is what *Compare* is for, a paragraph at a time: you cannot take
one person's edit and refuse another's inside the same paragraph.

## Limits

- A file from another tool, or one exported with markers off, falls back to title matching,
  which cannot recognize a renamed chapter.
